"GitHub - pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt: Empirical characterization of ICICLE NTT on consumer NVIDIA Blackwell (RTX 5070, sm_120), with a prototype for the digit-reversal bottleneck. · GitHub (https://github.laiyagushi.com/pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; pscamillo / icicle-blackwell-ntt Public ... Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n\npscamillo / icicle-blackwell-ntt Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 0\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 0\n  * Pull requests 0\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 0\n  * Insights\n\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nbenchmarks  | benchmarks  |    |  \n.gitignore  | .gitignore  |    |  \nLICENSE  | LICENSE  |    |  \nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \nREPORT.md  | REPORT.md  |    |  \n\n# ICICLE NTT on consumer Blackwell (RTX 5070, sm_120)\n\nAn independent, single-GPU empirical characterization of ICICLE v4.0.0's NTT on a consumer NVIDIA Blackwell GPU — where time actually goes, end to end and per kernel — plus a prototype that quantifies one recoverable bottleneck.\n\nShared in case it is useful to the ICICLE community and others bringing GPU ZK stacks to consumer Blackwell. Methodology is empirical before theoretical: every claim is a measurement, and the negative results are reported with the same weight as the positive one.\n\n> Full write-up with all profiles and interpretation: REPORT.md.\n\n## Findings (TL;DR)\n\n  1. ICICLE runs on Blackwell only through PTX-JIT. The shipped v4.0.0 CUDA backend embeds SASS up to sm_89 (Ada) plus `compute_89` PTX, but no sm_120 cubin; the driver JIT-compiles the PTX to sm_120 at first launch. This is more robust than most of the ecosystem (which ships no PTX and hard-fails on Blackwell), but it carries first-launch JIT latency and a PTX-forward-compatibility dependency.\n  2. No JIT throughput penalty on field arithmetic. A BabyBear Montgomery-modmul kernel built native sm_120 vs `compute_89`-PTX-then-JIT measured within run-to-run noise (1213 vs 1257 Gmodmul/s). A native build's value is removing JIT latency and the PTX dependency, not speeding up the arithmetic.\n  3. Butterflies are already at the memory ceiling; the digit-reversal pass is the bottleneck. At 2²², `ntt64` runs at 84.7% and `ntt32dit` at 76.5% of DRAM peak. The single largest kernel, `reorder_digits_and_normalize` (39% of NTT kernel time), runs at only 46% of peak with a global-store efficiency of 4 of 32 bytes per sector (12.5%) — the textbook scatter signature of digit reversal.\n  4. That headroom is recoverable (prototype). On the same GPU/size/dtype, a scattered bit-reversal reproducing ICICLE's exact 4/32 store signature runs at ~375 GB/s, while a shared-memory-staged coalesced permutation runs at ~780 GB/s (~2×). Since the reorder is 39% of NTT kernel time, a coalesced (COBRA-style) digit-reversal would translate to an estimated ~15–20% end-to-end NTT speedup.\n\n## Results at a glance\n\nMeasurement (RTX 5070, 2²² BabyBear)  | Result\n--- | ---\nmodmul — native sm_120 vs JIT compute_89  | 1212.8 vs 1256.9 Gmodmul/s (within noise)\n`ntt64` DRAM throughput  | 84.7% of peak\n`ntt32dit` DRAM throughput  | 76.5% of peak\n`reorder_digits_and_normalize` — share / DRAM / store eff.  | 39% of NTT time / 46.2% of peak / 4-of-32 bytes/sector\ncoalesced copy (bandwidth ceiling)  | ~1320 GB/s\nscattered bit-reversal (= ICICLE reorder pattern)  | ~375 GB/s\nshared-memory tiled transpose (coalesced permutation)  | ~780 GB/s (~2.1×)\nsingle-shot NTT over host data (2²²)  | ~74% PCIe, ~12% compute\n\n## Layout\n    \n    `benchmarks/\n      bench_modmul.cu     # Finding 2: native sm_120 vs JIT compute_89 modmul throughput\n      ntt_bench.cpp       # Findings 1 & 3: ICICLE NTT harness (links the v4 babybear frontend)\n      bitrev_bench.cu     # Finding 4: recoverable-bandwidth prototype (copy / scatter / transpose)\n    REPORT.md             # full characterization, profiles, and interpretation\n    `\n\n## Quickstart\n    \n    # Finding 2 — field-arithmetic JIT vs native (no ICICLE needed)\n    nvcc -O3 -arch=sm_120                                  benchmarks/bench_modmul.cu -o bench_native\n    nvcc -O3 -gencode arch=compute_89,code=compute_89      benchmarks/bench_modmul.cu -o bench_jit\n    ./bench_native 2048 ; ./bench_jit 2048\n    \n    # Finding 4 — recoverable-bandwidth prototype (no ICICLE needed)\n    nvcc -O3 -arch=sm_120 benchmarks/bitrev_bench.cu -o bitrev_bench\n    ./bitrev_bench\n    sudo -E $(which ncu) --kernel-name \"regex:k_copy|k_bitrev_scatter|k_transpose_tiled\" \\\n         -c 3 --section MemoryWorkloadAnalysis ./bitrev_bench\n    \n    # Findings 1 & 3 — ICICLE NTT harness (needs ICICLE v4 babybear frontend + CUDA backend)\n    #   see header of benchmarks/ntt_bench.cpp for the exact build/link line\n    export ICICLE_BACKEND_INSTALL_DIR=$HOME/ingo/icicle-cuda-backend/icicle/lib/backend\n    ./ntt_bench 24                                   # sweep 2^16..2^24\n    nsys profile --stats=true ./ntt_bench 22 22      # kernel + memcpy breakdown\n    sudo -E $(which ncu) --kernel-name \"regex:ntt32dit|ntt64|reorder_digits\" -c 6 --set basic ./ntt_bench 22 22\n\nEach harness is a single, correctness-gated file; build/run/profile commands are in its header comment.\n\n## Environment\n\nRTX 5070 (GB205, sm_120, 48 SMs, ~672 GB/s GDDR7, ~48 MB L2) · driver 575.57.08 / CUDA 12.9 · Ryzen 7 9800X3D, 128 GB · Ubuntu 24.04 (kernel 6.8) · ICICLE v4.0.0 (frontend from source, `FIELD=babybear`; CUDA backend `ubuntu22-cuda122`). All numbers are from this single machine.\n\n## License\n\nMIT — see LICENSE. The harnesses are original; they call ICICLE through its public API and do not redistribute it.\n\n## About\n\nEmpirical characterization of ICICLE NTT on consumer NVIDIA Blackwell (RTX 5070, sm_120), with a prototype for the digit-reversal bottleneck.\n\n### Topics\n\ncryptographycudagpugpu-computingiciclenttnvidia-blackwellperformanceprofilingzero-knowledge\n\n### Resources\n\nReadme\n\nMIT license\n\n\n### Stars\n\n0 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n0 forks--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Unified Arena for LLM Agents across Diverse Eurogame · Harness Hub (https://www.harness-hub.org/papers/a-unified-arena-for-llm-agents-across-diverse-eurogame)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; arXiv:2610.35773 cs.AI · Oct 26, 2026\n\narXiv:2610.35773cs.AI · Oct 26, 2026\n\n# A Unified Arena for LLM Agents across Diverse Eurogame\n\nFengrui Zhang, Xiang Li, Ruomeng Ding, Jiayi Yuan, Yuchen Huang, Zhaoyang Han, Xiaoliang Wang, Daoyuan Chen, Yaliang Li\n\nRead on arXiv\n\n## Abstract\n\nInteractive environments offer a practical way to evaluate large language model (LLM) agents, and competitive games extend this evaluation to decisions shaped by competing goals and other agents' choices. Yet existing evaluations confined to individual games or execution frameworks leave unclear which competitive advantages persist across settings. We introduce DECE (Diverse Eurogame Competitive Environments), a unified arena spanning ten Eurogames with shared interfaces and evaluation protocols. These games combine lasting strategic commitments, competition over shared resources, and multiple routes to victory. Our primary study evaluates 12 model-harness configurations, spanning nine models and four execution harnesses, in 480 three-player matches across ten games. The main and supplementary studies comprise 1,032 completed matches in total. These comparisons reveal broad cross-game ranking agreement alongside game-specific strengths. Matched studies show that the same model can perform differently across execution harnesses, adding another source of variation to these profiles. These performance advantages do not consistently translate into better token or cost efficiency, making resource use an additional consideration when comparing configurations. Together, the findings support evaluating complete agent configurations by both their competitive performance across games and the resources needed to achieve it.\n\n## Details\n\narXiv ID 2610.35773\n\nPublished Oct 26, 2026\n\nRevised Oct 26, 2026\n\nPrimary category cs.AI\n\n#harness#evaluation#agents#llm--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSriram Sridhar (https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~srirams/)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; SAK: Sparse Arguments of Knowledge, from Sparse Lookup Arguments\n\nImage: Profile Picture\n\n# Sriram Sridhar\n\nsrirams@berkeley.edu\n\n## About Me\n\nI'm a PhD student in Computer Science at UC Berkeley, advised by Sanjam Garg. My research interests lie in cryptography, with a focus on efficient zero-knowledge proofs, their building blocks, and applications in multi-party computation protocols.\n\nI graduated with a B.S. (Research) in Mathematics from the Indian Institute of Science in 2021.\n\n## Research\n\n*denotes non-alphabetical ordering of authors\n\nSAK: Sparse Arguments of Knowledge, from Sparse Lookup Arguments\nAbhiram Kothapalli, Sriram Sridhar, Arantxa Zapico\n[ePrint]\n\nMist: Transparent Polynomial Commitments from Class Group Subset-Product Accumulators\nSriram Sridhar\n[ePrint]\n\nRelaxed Modular PCS from Arbitrary PCS and Applications to SNARKs for Integers*\nAlireza Shirzad, Sriram Sridhar, Dimitrios Papadopoulos, Charalampos Papamanthou\n[TCC'26] [ZKProof 8] [ePrint]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSelective Transfer through Sequence Distillation:… - arXiv (https://arxiv-troller.com/paper/3350587/)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; arXiv ID ... October 19, 2026\n\n# Selective Transfer through Sequence Distillation: From Benchmark Knowledge to Backdoor Behaviour\n\nAuthors: Jonibek Mansurov, Sholpan Bolatzhanova, Filbert Aurelian Tjiaranta, Vallerie Alexandara Putra, Alham Fikri Aji\n\narXiv ID\n\n2610.26311\n\nSubmitted\n\nOctober 19, 2026\n\nLast Updated\n\nOctober 20, 2026\n\nCategories\n\ncs.AI\n\n### Abstract\n\nKnowledge distillation is widely used to transfer capabilities from teacher models to student models, but recent work shows that it can also transfer unintended teacher properties. We study whether different hidden teacher properties survive the same distillation channels. Specifically, we compare two properties: benchmark-associated knowledge and refusal backdoors, and show that distillation treats them very differently. Teachers fine-tuned on benchmark data successfully pass that knowledge to students through all four distillation channels we test, including when students are trained only on teacher-generated number sequences containing no natural language or domain content whatsoever. Refusal backdoors tell the opposite story: backdoored teachers achieve near-perfect triggered refusal, yet sequence-distilled students inherit essentially none of this behaviour. Logit-based distillation transfers it substantially, reaching up to 85.5% triggered refusal in students that saw zero backdoor examples. Distillation is therefore selective, and the selection criterion is not what data the student sees, but what structure the teacher's property has. The code is available at https://github.com/MJonibek/kd-leakage.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBridging Exploration and Persona: Information-Directed Dueling for Test-Time Agent Personalization (arXiv:2610.24385) | Papers with Code (https://paperswithcode.co/paper/2610.24385)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 6 days ago; Paper · arXiv:2610.24385 · 17 October 2026\nPaper · arXiv:2610.24385 · 17 October 2026\n\n# Bridging Exploration and Persona: Information-Directed Dueling for Test-Time Agent Personalization\n\nJie Mao, Pingchen Lu, Xiangyi Wang, Hengquan Huang, Shuang Qiu, Min Zhang, Zhongxiang Dai\n\n## TL;DR\n\nID-TAP selects personas through information-directed dueling rather than fixed exploration. A query-conditioned utility model shares evidence across related queries, while Bradley–Terry updates score hypothetical challengers; it leads across seven benchmarks within ten comparison rounds.\n\n## Abstract\n\nEffective test-time personalization requires LLM agents to adapt to query-dependent user preferences from lightweight interaction. We study persona selection with binary comparisons between candidate responses as the online signal. We present ID-TAP, a scalable, IDS-inspired neural dueling-bandit framework for large discrete persona spaces. ID-TAP combines a query-conditioned neural utility model with a lightweight Bradley-Terry explorer, and transfers comparison evidence across related queries through similarity-weighted warm starts. The neural model shares statistical strength across queries while preserving query-specific scores, and efficient BT updates score hypothetical challengers. Across seven user-conditioned generation benchmarks under reference- and model-based preference simulators, ID-TAP achieves the strongest aggregate performance among the evaluated baselines within a fixed ten-round comparison budget. These results support information-directed challenger selection as an effective design for test-time persona adaptation.\n\n## Paper resources\n\n  * ArXiv abstract\n  * PDF\n\n### Code\n\n  * s1412/ID-TAP\n\n### Tasks\n\n  * Agents\n\n### Methods\n\n  * Large language model (LLM)\n  * Test-Time Adaptation (TTA)\n\n### Organizations\n\n  * Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen\n  * City University of Hong Kong--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Token Games: Evaluating Language Model Reasoning with Puzzle Duels · mlsanity (https://mlsanity.com/paper/2602.17831/)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; arXiv 2602.17831 ↗First posted 19 Feb 2026 cs.AI cited by 1 ... Cited by 1 paper in the map (data through 26 Oct 2026).\n\n# The Token Games: Evaluating Language Model Reasoning with Puzzle Duels\n\narXiv 2602.17831 ↗First posted 19 Feb 2026 cs.AI cited by 1\n\nAbstract (arXiv)\n\nEvaluating the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models is increasingly challenging as models improve. Human curation of hard questions is highly expensive, especially in recent benchmarks using PhD-level domain knowledge to challenge the most capable models. Even then, there is always a concern about whether these questions test genuine reasoning or if similar problems have been seen during training. Here, we take inspiration from 16th-century mathematical duels to design The Token Games (TTG): an evaluation framework where models challenge each other by creating their own puzzles. We leverage the format of Programming Puzzles - given a function that returns a boolean, find inputs that make it return True - to flexibly represent problems and enable verifying solutions. Using results from pairwise duels, we then compute Elo ratings, allowing us to compare models relative to each other. We evaluate 10 frontier models on TTG, and closely match the ranking from existing benchmarks such as Humanity's Last Exam, spending less than 200 USD and without involving any human effort in creating puzzles. We also find that creating good puzzles is still a highly challenging task for current models. Overall, our work suggests new paradigms for evaluating reasoning that avoid saturation by design, and that allow testing models for other skills like creativity and task creation alongside problem solving.\n\nKey facts. Cited by 1 paper in the map (data through 26 Oct 2026). Placed in Reasoning benchmarks and evaluation, one of 17 directions in LLM reasoning. Builds on 22 of its 32 references that are in the map. First posted on arXiv 19 Feb 2026.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSWE-Duel: Towards a Self-Scaling Arena for Coding Agents |... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/swe-duel-towards-a-self-scaling-arena-for-coding-agents-5062-731c8853)\nciteturn4search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Back ArXiv SWE-Duel: Towards a Self-Scaling Arena for Coding Agents ... License: arXiv preprint\n\nBack ArXiv SWE-Duel: Towards a Self-Scaling Arena for Coding Agents\n\nArXiv\n\n# SWE-Duel: Towards a Self-Scaling Arena for Coding Agents\n\nZhensu Sun, David Lo, Lwin Khin Shar, Wei Minn\n\narXiv:2610.12569 Published Oct 8, 2026\n\nChat with paper Cite 中文 View PDF Share\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: Oct 08, 2026\n\n### Metrics\n\nCode: GitHub\n\nSource: ArXiv\n\n### Paper Preview\n\n### Abstract\n\nStatic coding benchmarks are fixed at release. Over time their tasks leak into training data, and stronger agents eventually saturate them. We argue that a benchmark should instead aim to be self-scaling: its tasks and oracles are created at evaluation time, so no content exists in advance to leak, and its difficulty is set by the participating agents rather than fixed at release. We introduce SWE-Duel, an arena in which two coding agents evaluate each other over mirrored Red-Blue turns. In each turn, the Red agent works on a real repository across three context-isolated sessions. It proposes and implements a new feature with feature tests, then embeds a subtle bug in that feature along with sealed bug-triggering tests. The resulting pull request is admitted only if (1) it passes the feature tests and the repository's existing tests while failing exactly the bug tests, and (2) a fresh session of the same agent can repair the bug blind, which rules out unsolvable tasks. The Blue agent then reviews the pull request without being told a bug exists. It wins only if its revision passes all three test suites, and then the two agents swap roles. We run a tournament among 11 model-harness participants on 15 repositories in five languages for 1,500 turns. The leaders win in different ways. GPT-5.5 with Codex CLI ranks first as the strongest solver, repairing 98.3% of the defects it reviews. Claude Opus 4.8 with mini-swe-agent ranks second as the strongest task author, with a 27.3% Red win rate. Finally, within one model family, tasks written by the newest version defeat a fixed-reference solver far more often than those of older versions (42.9% vs. 4.0%), giving preliminary evidence that task difficulty scales with task authoring capability.\n\n### Paper Details\n\narXiv ID: arXiv:2610.12569\n\nLicense: arXiv preprint\n\nView on arXiv Download PDF\n\n### Authors\n\nZS Zhensu Sun 9 papers DL David Lo 3 papers LK Lwin Khin Shar 54 papers WM Wei Minn 6 papers--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOnline Convex Optimization with Dueling Feedback — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/online-convex-optimization-with-dueling-feedback)\nciteturn4search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;     @misc{lu2026online, ...       url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15050},\n\nBibTeX\n    \n    @misc{lu2026online,\n      author = {Yiyang Lu and Hareshkumar Jadav and Mohammad Pedramfar and Ranveer Singh and Vaneet Aggarwal},\n      title = {{Online Convex Optimization with Dueling Feedback}},\n      year = {2026},\n      eprint = {2608.15050},\n      archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n      url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15050},\n    }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCost-Aware Best-LLM Identification using Dueling Feedback | Arxiv - DeepPaper (https://arxiv.deeppaper.ai/papers/2609.30360v1)\nciteturn4search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; Image: arXiv 2609.30360v1 ... Sep 24, 2026\n\n# Cost-Aware Best-LLM Identification using Dueling Feedback\n\nPDF DeepRead\n\n### Key Contributions\n\nFormulated a cost-aware best-arm identification problem for LLMs, proposed a theoretically grounded Track-and-Stop algorithm, and proved its asymptotic optimality.\n\n### Abstract\n\nInspired by the problem of identifying the best model from a collection of large language models (LLMs) with heterogeneous querying costs, we formulate and analyse a variant of the multi-armed bandit (MAB) with (i) dueling feedback, where pairwise comparisons between model responses provide robust preference signals, and (ii) heterogeneous sampling costs, reflecting the differing costs of querying different LLMs. Assuming the existence of a Condorcet winner, a condition we empirically validate across multiple real-world datasets, we propose a Track-and-Stop style algorithm for best-arm identification with prescribed confidence. We prove that the algorithm almost surely achieves the asymptotically optimal cost as the error tends to zero. Finally, we extensively evaluate our approach on both synthetic and real-world instances, demonstrating consistent improvements over classical cost-unaware algorithms and their cost-aware extensions.\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper introduces a cost-aware multi-armed bandit algorithm using dueling feedback to efficiently identify the best LLM while minimizing total query costs.\n\n### Subjects\n\ncs.LG cs.AI stat.ML\n\n### arXiv\n\nImage: arXiv 2609.30360v1\n\n### Paper Type\n\nTheory Method\n\n### Domain\n\nAI LLM Optimization\n\nAuthors\n\nSarvesh Gharat, Nikhil Karamchandani, Jayakrishnan Nair\n\nSubmitted\n\nSep 24, 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv Troller (https://arxiv-troller.com/?q=paper%3A+2609.23015)\nciteturn4search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; arXiv:2610.36776 ... Submitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nDependency Graphs of Mathematical Arguments: Representation, Structural Measurement, and Proof Review\n\nEunji Park\n\narXiv:2610.36776\n\nAuthors: Eunji Park\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nWe propose a source-grounded framework for representing and measuring mathematical arguments with assistance from large language models. It includes numbered and unnumbered statements, equations, assumptions, and constructions, distinguishing source occurrences from contextual states and inference records. Proposed dependencies retain source evidence and review status. A measurement contract fixes manuscript versions, boundaries, granularity, proof choices, and macro anchors. On a selected finite directed acyclic graph, depth is the longest prerequisite-path length, and bulk counts distinct ancestor edges. We give a union recurrence that accounts for shared subarguments and identify reachability conditions preserving depth and the macro skeleton; unrestricted refinement need not preserve depth or bulk. \narXiv:2610.33690\n\nAuthors: Shu Miao, Feiming Wang, Wentao Long, Chenyi Li, Kun Yuan, Zaiwen Wen\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nMany mathematical research agents focus on solving problems with fixed statements. However, conjecture research, especially in applied mathematics, may require revising candidate questions or conclusions as investigation proceeds, coupling problem formulation with proof development. We present PACE, a research-proof agent that supports this process through a structured research loop that selects mathematical objectives and develops or challenges candidate mechanisms. The whole prover constructs proofs from these mechanisms, and an independent verifier checks them against the original target. Evidence-tracking memory stores assumptions and dependencies arising during research and marks evidence for revalidation when a candidate or condition changes. This memory supports task-specific context composition that keeps checked premises, exploratory material and unresolved obligations distinct. To evaluate proof construction and conjecture research, we introduce ResearchProofBench, an applied-mathematics benchmark focused on optimization, comprising 51 known-proof tasks with 331 hidden checkpoints under historical abstract access and six open-problem cases. The known-proof tasks assess reconstruction of published results, while the open-problem cases assess mathematical investigation and proof development. On the 51 known-proof tasks, PACE achieves an aggregate Proof Score of 75.16 and completes 20 proofs. In conjecture research, PACE develops conditional results, with its submissions receiving higher review scores than the compared baselines.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nD1.3 Action Plans for 6 CHESS (https://chess-eu.cs.ut.ee/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/D1.3_Action_Plans_for_6_CHESS_Challenge_Areas.pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; • Organization of EUROPEN’26 security workshop in October 2026 with planned\nD1.3 Action Plans for 6 CHESS Challenge Research Areas \n \nunder Czech Ministry of Interior funding call. Options for resubmission of CoinJoin \ninvestigation project are investigated. \n \nAction 2: Organising events  \n• Presentation of improved ZkLogin implementation adapted to ECDSA and extended \nwith hardware-hardened Salt service and Alphabil blockchain is planned for CHESS \nconsortium meeting in Tallinn in January 2026. \n• The threshold cryptography demonstrator with MeeSign platform extended as part of \nCA4 (cooperation between MUNI and CYBER) was proposed to RIA for demo day \ndissemination event in March 2026 in Tallinn.  \n• The SP2I conference will be organized 2026 along with the CA1 to propagate the \nresults from CA4. \n• Organization of EUROPEN’26 security workshop in October 2026 with planned \ndissemination of CA4 results. \nAction 3: Participation at conferences \n \n• We will participate in the SP2I’26 and ETACS’26 conferences (co-organized by \nCHESS), the International Digital Twin Hub Community (https://digitaltwinhub.co.uk), \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCUDA-X Crypto Infrastructure: What NVIDIA's GPUs Do (https://cryptoaimeta.com/ai/cuda-x-crypto-infrastructure/)\nciteturn4search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; Ingonyama’s ICICLE is the clearest example of CUDA-adjacent infrastructure doing real work here: an open-source, CUDA-based GPU library purpose-built to accelerate MSM, NTT, elliptic-curve operations, and Poseidon hashing for ZK applications, with published benchmarks showing GPU-based speedups of up to 258x for large batches versus CPU baselines, and full zkEVM proof generation compressed to roughly 58 seconds in one cited implementation. ... Render kicked off 2026 with the production release of Octane 2026, OTOY’s flagship rendering engine, and founder Jules Urbach has used NVIDIA’s GTC conference to detail deeper integration with Blackwell-generation hardware and Omniverse tooling.\n\n## Where CUDA-X Actually Earns Its Keep: Zero-Knowledge Proof Generation\n\nIf there’s one place inside core blockchain infrastructure — not adjacent infrastructure — where GPU acceleration is a genuine, growing dependency, it’s zero-knowledge proof generation for rollups and privacy-preserving chains. ZK-proof systems spend the overwhelming majority of their compute time on two operations, multi-scalar multiplication (MSM) and the number-theoretic transform (NTT), both embarrassingly parallel — exactly the kind of math GPUs were built for.\n\nIngonyama’s ICICLE is the clearest example of CUDA-adjacent infrastructure doing real work here: an open-source, CUDA-based GPU library purpose-built to accelerate MSM, NTT, elliptic-curve operations, and Poseidon hashing for ZK applications, with published benchmarks showing GPU-based speedups of up to 258x for large batches versus CPU baselines, and full zkEVM proof generation compressed to roughly 58 seconds in one cited implementation. ICICLE isn’t an NVIDIA product — it’s third-party tooling built on CUDA because CUDA is the mature, well-documented layer for exploiting GPU parallelism, exactly the relationship CUDA-X is designed to enable across any industry that adopts it.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElectronic Colloquium on Computational Complexity, Report No. 28 (2026) (https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/028/download/)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Reductions from Public-Coin ZK . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... 25\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReceived:  March 27, 2026.     Revised: May 28, 2026.                                                                                                     856 (https://oaji.net/articles/2026/3603-1781922064.pdf)\nciteturn4search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; International Journal of Intelligent Engineering and Systems, Vol.19, No.7, 2026           DOI: 10.22266/ijies2026.0731.49 ... License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMathDuels: Evaluating LLMs as Problem Posers and Solvers | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2604.21916)\nciteturn4search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; ## #1 MathDuels: Evaluating LLMs as Problem Posers and Solvers [PDF^{3}] [Copy] [Kimi^{2}] [REL] ... Publish: 2026-04-23 17:57:46 UTC\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZKExpress\n(2025 Q4)\nEdited by KurtPan\nKurtPan@ ZKP (https://express.zkpunk.pro/25q4.pdf)\nciteturn4search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; [BM25] Threshold Signatures from One-Way Functions . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... 1.6\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJesseQ: Efficient Zero-Knowledge Proofs for Circuits over Any Field (https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/533.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; improved by QuickSilver [6] to support any field. ... State-of-the-art implementations [6],\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElectronic Colloquium on Computational Complexity, Report No. 51 (2026) (https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/051/download/)\nciteturn4search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; 6\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt: Empirical characterization of ICICLE NTT on consumer NVIDIA Blackwell (RTX 5070, sm_120), with a prototype for the digit-reversal bottleneck. · GitHub (https://github.com/pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt\",\"lineno\":200}); Total lines: 297\nL161: ### Forks\nL162: \nL163: 【84†0 forks】\nL164: \nL165: 【85†Report repository】\nL166: \nL167: main\nL168: \nL169: 【86†Branches】【87†Tags】\nL170: \nL171: [Input: Go to file]\nL172: \nL173: Go to file\nL174: \nL175: Code\nL176: \nL177: Open more actions menu\nL178: \nL179: ## Latest commit\nL180: \nL181: \nL182: \nL183: ## History\nL184: \nL185: 1 Commit\nL186: \nL187: ## Folders and files\nL188: \nL189: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL190: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL191: 【88†benchmarks】  | 【88†benchmarks】  |    |\nL192: 【89†.gitignore】  | 【89†.gitignore】  |    |\nL193: 【90†LICENSE】  | 【90†LICENSE】  |    |\nL194: 【91†README.md】  | 【91†README.md】  |    |\nL195: 【92†REPORT.md】  | 【92†REPORT.md】  |    |\nL196: ## Repository files navigation\nL197: \nL198:   *   * 【93†README】\nL199:   * 【93†MIT license】\nL200: \nL201: More items\nL202: # ICICLE NTT on consumer Blackwell (RTX 5070, sm_120)\nL203: \nL204: An independent, single-GPU empirical characterization of 【94†ICICLE】 v4.0.0's NTT on a consumer NVIDIA Blackwell GPU — where time actually goes, end to end and per kernel — plus a prototype that quantifies one recoverable bottleneck.\nL205: Shared in case it is useful to the ICICLE community and others bringing GPU ZK stacks to consumer Blackwell. Methodology is empirical before theoretical: every claim is a measurement, and the negative results are reported with the same weight as the positive one.\nL206: \nL207: > Full write-up with all profiles and interpretation: 【92†REPORT.md】.\nL208: ## Findings (TL;DR)\nL209:   1. ICICLE runs on Blackwell only through PTX-JIT. The shipped v4.0.0 CUDA backend embeds SASS up to sm_89 (Ada) plus `compute_89` PTX, but no sm_120 cubin; the driver JIT-compiles the PTX to sm_120 at first launch. This is more robust than most of the ecosystem (which ships no PTX and hard-fails on Blackwell), but it carries first-launch JIT latency and a PTX-forward-compatibility dependency.\n"
